t candidates\" - maybe replacement refers to replacing a subtree with a single node (like tree editing). But here we only have removal/retention.\n\nGiven the provided fixtures, the simple algorithm (retain all non-removable nodes, ignore required symbols because they are already covered) yields the output. But to be robust, we implement the full algorithm.\n\nWe also need to consider that non-removable nodes are already retained, so they cover the root. The root is required. So we always have root.\n\nThus, the solution: \n- Build node dict.\n- Identify root (parent=None).\n- retained = set of ids of nodes with removable==False.\n- For each required symbol s:\n    - If any node in retained has symbol s, continue.\n    - Else, find all nodes with symbol s and removable==True. Choose the one with maximum utility (if tie, any deterministic, e.g., first encountered by id).\n    - If found, add that node and all its ancestors (up to root) to retained set.\n- Optionally, we might need to ensure that the retained set is connected? By adding ancestors, it becomes connected. But if a non-removable node is under a removable node that we haven't retained, that would be disconnected. However, non-removable nodes are always retained, so we must ensure their ancestors are also retained. This is a critical point: if a non-removable node has a removable parent, we must retain that parent to keep the non-removable node connected. The current algorithm doesn't handle that. In the fixtures, all non-removable nodes have non-removable ancestors? root is non-removable, n1, n3, n5 are non-removable, and their parents are: n1 parent root (non-removable), n3 parent root, n5 parent n3 (non-removable). So ancestors are non-removable. But consider a scenario where a non-removable node is child of a removable node. Then we must retain that removable ancestor to keep the tree connected. So we should ensure all ancestors of any retained node are also retained. So after initial retained set (non-removable nodes), we should propagate up the parent chain to include all ancestors for each retained node. This will automatically connect the tree.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n1. Find all node dicts. Build map id->node.\n2. For each node, also store children list for convenience (not necessary).\n3. Initialize retained_ids = set()\n4. Add all nodes where removable==False to retained_ids.\n5. For each node in retained_ids (starting with the initial set), ensure all ancestors (parent, parent's parent, etc.) are added to retained_ids. This can be done via a loop or recursion.\n6. Then, for each required_symbol in required_symbols:\n   - If there exists a node in retained_ids with that symbol, continue.\n   - Else, find the best removable node (utility max) with that symbol. Add that node to retained_ids, and then add all its ancestors to retained_ids (if not already).\n7. Re-check required symbols? Actually after adding one node, it might cover multiple symbols, but since we iterate over required_symbols list, and after adding, later symbols might already be covered. So it's fine.\n8. After processing all required symbols, the retained_ids set is final.\n9. Removed_ids = set(all node ids) - retained_ids.\n10. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": sorted list of removed_ids? Not required to be sorted, but deterministic. We'll sort for consistency. Actually the order of arrays doesn't matter but we can output in the order they appear in the input nodes list for determinism. But simpler to use sorted.\n\nBut note: The algorithm above might add a removable node for a required symbol even if that node is already in retained_ids due to ancestor propagation? That would be fine because we check if any retained node has the symbol.\n\nEdge cases: What if a required symbol is not present in any node? Possibly then we cannot satisfy; but we assume valid input.\n\nAlso, what if there is a required symbol that is only present in a node that is non-removable? Already covered.\n\nNow implement solve(case) function.\n\nI'll write Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = case[\"required_symbols\"]\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    retained = set()\n    # Initially, all non-removable nodes\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            retained.add(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Ensure ancestors of all retained nodes are retained (tree connectivity)\n    # We need to propagate up the parent chain for each retained node that might have missing ancestors.\n    # Since adding ancestors may cascade, we can do a loop until stable.\n    changed = True\n    while changed:\n        changed = False\n        for nid in list(retained):\n            node = node_map[nid]\n            parent_id = node[\"parent\"]\n            while parent_id is not None:\n                if parent_id not in retained:\n                    retained.add(parent_id)\n                    changed = True\n                parent_id = node_map[parent_id][\"parent\"]  # climb further up? Actually we need to update node reference for each step. Better: climb iteratively.\n        # Alternative: simpler to just handle each node's parent chain once.\n        # Since we may have added new nodes, we need to check again; but with this loop it's safe.\n\n    # Actually easier: after initial set, we can recursively add ancestors for all nodes in retained.\n    # Implement function to ensure ancestors.\n    def ensure_ancestors(nid):\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        parent_id = node[\"parent\"]\n        while parent_id is not None:\n            if parent_id in retained:\n                break\n            retained.add(parent_id)\n            parent_id = node_map[parent_id][\"parent\"]\n    # Call for all current retained\n    for nid in list(retained):\n        ensure_ancestors(nid)\n    # But ensure_ancestors may add new nodes that themselves have missing ancestors? The while loop ensures that we add all missing ancestors up to root or until we hit a node already in retained. So once we add a new ancestor, we don't need to check its ancestors because the loop would continue. However, the loop in ensure_ancestors already climbs up until root or existing retained. So it's sufficient.\n\n    # Then handle required_symbols\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        # check if any retained node has this symbol\n        if any(node_map[nid][\"symbol\"] == sym for nid in retained):\n            continue\n        # find best removable node with symbol sym\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == sym and n[\"removable\"]]\n        if not candidates:\n            # maybe impossible? but skip\n            continue\n        # pick max utility\n        best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n        # add best and its ancestors\n        retained.add(best[\"id\"])\n        ensure_ancestors(best[\"id\"])\n        # Note: ensure_ancestors will add ancestors, which may add other nodes that could also have the required symbol, but we already checked that no retained node had it; after adding, we are done for this symbol.\n\n    # Now compute removed\n    all_ids = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = all_ids - retained\n    # Return as lists (order doesn't matter but we can sort for deterministic output)\n    removed_list = sorted(removed)\n    retained_list = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_list, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_list}\n\nCheck: The first fixture, after initial retained: root, n1, n3, n5. ensure_ancestors: all ancestors already in retained. Then for symbol s1, retained already has n1, so skip. So retained = root, n1, n3, n5. Removed = n2, n4, n6. matches.\n\nNow output must be JSON serializable. We'll use json.dumps? But the function must return a Python dict (which will be JSON-serializable). The evaluator expects a JSON value; returning a dict is fine.\n\nNow encode the source as JSON string for the submission envelope. The 